    "bottomLine": "The default choice for most households that want certified lead reduction without a high running cost.",
    "description": "Brita's Elite filter, formerly sold as Longlast+, is the reason this pitcher ranks first. The cartridge is rated for 120 gallons, roughly six months for a typical household, and the performance data sheet lists certified reduction of lead along with chlorine, several industrial chemicals, some pesticides and particulates under NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401. That combination of a health certification and a long rating keeps cost per gallon among the lowest for any lead-certified pitcher. The 10-cup body fits most fridge doors, and the lid indicator tracks cartridge age. The trade-off is speed: water moves through the denser media more slowly than through Brita's basic white cartridge, and owners commonly report waits of several minutes per refill. Usable filtered volume per fill is closer to five or six cups, as with any reservoir pitcher. If you only want better taste, a cheaper standard cartridge works, but for older homes with lead risk this is the pick most people should start with."
