        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#certifications" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Reading Certifications</a>
          <a href="#what-to-remove" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Match the Filter to Your Water</a>
          <a href="#cost-per-gallon" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Real Cost Per Gallon</a>
          <a href="#filter-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Carbon vs Ion Exchange vs Membrane</a>
          <a href="#daily-use" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Flow, Capacity and Fridge Fit</a>
          <a href="#filter-changes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. When to Change Cartridges</a>
          <a href="#pitcher-limits" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. What a Pitcher Cannot Do</a>
          <a href="#cleaning" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cleaning and Hygiene</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people buy a filter pitcher because the tap water tastes of chlorine, and almost any pitcher fixes that. The harder question is lead, PFAS and other contaminants you cannot taste, and there the gap between cartridges is enormous. The rule that settles it: read the performance data sheet, find NSF/ANSI 53 and 401 claims for the specific contaminant you care about, and ignore every word on the front of the box.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Water Filter Pitchers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are ranked on published certifications (NSF/ANSI 42, 53, 401 and 473, verified on each brand's performance data sheet), rated cartridge life, replacement cost and patterns in owner feedback about flow speed, leaks and fit. No lab work was done for this list; every claim is attributed to the label or the listing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Read a Filter Certification
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pitcher on the shelf says it makes water cleaner. Only a handful can prove it for the contaminants that affect health, and the proof lives in a document most buyers never open: the <strong>performance data sheet</strong>. Brands are required to publish it for certified claims, and it lists each contaminant, the influent challenge level used in the test, and the percentage reduction achieved. If a pitcher brand does not make this sheet easy to find, treat its marketing claims as unverified.
          </p>

          <p>
            The standards you will see are numbered. <strong>NSF/ANSI 42</strong> covers aesthetic effects such as chlorine taste and odor and particulates. It says nothing about health. <strong>NSF/ANSI 53</strong> covers health-related contaminants, including lead, mercury, cysts and certain volatile organic chemicals, and also PFOA and PFOS under newer listings. <strong>NSF/ANSI 401</strong> covers emerging compounds such as some pharmaceuticals, pesticides and BPA. <strong>NSF/ANSI 473</strong> is the dedicated PFOA and PFOS standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification is per contaminant, not per product. A cartridge can be certified to Standard 53 for cysts and nothing else, and the box can still carry the Standard 53 number. That is why a pitcher that says NSF 53 on the front might do nothing about lead. Look for the word lead, or PFOA and PFOS, beside a reduction percentage on the data sheet before you trust the claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who certified it also matters. NSF International, IAPMO, the Water Quality Association and CSA are all accredited certifiers, and a listing from any of them means the product was tested by a third party and the factory is audited. A phrase such as <strong>tested to NSF standards</strong> is weaker: it can mean the brand paid a lab to run the protocol once without ongoing audits. That is not worthless, but it is not the same as a certification you can look up in a public listing.
          </p>

