        <div id="what-to-remove" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Match the Filter to What Is in Your Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before choosing a pitcher, find out what your water actually contains. If you are on a public system, your utility mails or posts an annual <strong>Consumer Confidence Report</strong> listing detected contaminants and their levels. Search your utility name plus CCR. If you rent or live in an older house, the report will not show lead from your own service line or plumbing, because lead usually enters at the house, not at the treatment plant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homes built before 1986 can have lead solder on copper pipes, and many pre-1950 homes still have lead service lines. In that situation a certified lead filter is one of the cheapest health purchases you can make, and the right pick is a cartridge certified to Standard 53 for lead at the 150 parts per billion challenge level. Many local health departments and utilities offer free or low-cost lead testing kits, which give you a baseline before and after you start filtering.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your concern is PFAS, look for Standard 53 or 473 claims for PFOA and PFOS specifically. The EPA finalized enforceable drinking water limits for several PFAS compounds in 2024, and many utilities are still working toward compliance, so a pitcher can be a sensible bridge. If the issue is just taste, chlorine smell or a bit of sediment, a basic Standard 42 carbon cartridge solves it for the least money and the fastest pour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Well owners have a different problem. Bacteria, nitrates and arsenic are the main private-well risks, and most pitchers are not certified for bacteria or nitrate at all. A pitcher is not a substitute for testing a well at least once a year through a state-certified lab. If those tests find a problem, the fix is usually a point-of-entry system or a reverse osmosis unit, which our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home water filtration system guide</a> covers in detail.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Taste problem: any Standard 42 cartridge. Lead or PFAS: Standard 53 or 473 for that exact contaminant.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy the certification that matches your water report, then choose among pitchers that hold it on size, speed and cost. Buying the most expensive pitcher without checking the report wastes money as often as it helps.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-gallon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Real Cost Per Gallon
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pitcher itself is a one-time purchase of roughly $20 to $90 at the time of writing. The cartridges are the real cost, and they vary more than most buyers expect. A cheap pitcher with a 40-gallon cartridge that costs $8 to $10 runs about 20 to 25 cents per gallon. A pricier pitcher whose cartridge is rated for 100 to 120 gallons can land closer to 15 cents per gallon even though each replacement costs more up front.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work out your own number. A household of two drinking about a gallon a day, plus coffee and cooking, goes through 40 gallons in five or six weeks. A family of four can do it in under three weeks. That means a 40-gallon cartridge can cost a busy household $150 to $200 a year, while a 120-gallon cartridge rated for six months often halves that. Buying filters in multipacks of three to six typically trims another 15 to 25 percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            ZeroWater is the exception to simple math. Its ion exchange cartridge lasts in proportion to how much dissolved mineral is in your water. With soft water of around 50 parts per million total dissolved solids, owners commonly report 25 to 40 gallons per cartridge. With hard water above 300 ppm, that can fall to 8 to 15 gallons, which pushes cost per gallon above 60 cents. Check your hardness before committing to that system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare all of this with bottled water at roughly $1 to $1.50 per gallon for multipacks and $5 or more for single bottles, and every pitcher here pays for itself within weeks. Compare it with an under-sink filter at roughly 5 to 10 cents per gallon over its life, and you can see why households that drink a lot of water often graduate to a sink system after a year or two.
          </p>

