        <div id="filter-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Carbon vs Ion Exchange vs Membrane
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every pitcher cartridge starts with <strong>activated carbon</strong>. Carbon has a huge internal surface area that grabs chlorine, many organic chemicals and the compounds that make water taste off. Granular carbon, the loose black beads in older cartridges, works quickly but has gaps water can slip through. Pressed or bonded carbon blocks force water through a tighter matrix, which improves contaminant contact and is why the better lead and PFAS cartridges use them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ion exchange resin</strong> swaps undesirable ions such as lead, copper and the minerals that make hard water for something harmless. Most mixed cartridges pair resin with carbon so they handle both metals and taste. ZeroWater takes it further with a five-stage design that strips nearly all dissolved solids, which is why its included meter reads close to zero when the filter is fresh. That also removes calcium and magnesium, so the water tastes flat to some people.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Membrane microfilters</strong> such as the one in LifeStraw pitchers physically block particles above a stated pore size. They are aimed at bacteria, parasites and microplastics, which matters more for well or travel water than for treated city supply. Membranes clog gradually rather than failing suddenly, and flow slows as they load up. They work alongside a carbon stage, not instead of one.
          </p>

          <p>
            No pitcher uses reverse osmosis, because RO needs household water pressure to push water through its membrane and a gravity pitcher has almost none. If you need RO-level reduction of fluoride, nitrate, arsenic or total dissolved solids, a countertop or under-sink RO unit is the realistic step up. Pitcher cartridges that claim to rival RO usually mean they score well on a few contaminants, not across the board.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="daily-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Flow Speed, Capacity and Fridge Fit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flow speed is the complaint owners raise most, and it trades directly against filtration depth. A basic carbon cartridge typically fills a reservoir in two to five minutes. A dense carbon block certified for lead and PFAS can take 10 to 15 minutes per refill, and owners commonly report it slows further toward the end of a cartridge. If nobody in the house will wait, you will end up drinking straight from the tap, which defeats the purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity labels are misleading because the cup count includes the unfiltered top reservoir. A 10-cup pitcher usually yields about 5 to 6 cups of filtered water per fill. For one or two people that is fine. For a family, either refill constantly or look at a dispenser format: a 18- to 27-cup tank that sits on a fridge shelf with a spigot, where the slow flow matters less because you only refill it once or twice a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure your fridge door before buying. Most door shelves fit pitchers about 5 inches deep and up to 11 inches tall, and many larger models do not fit. A pitcher that has to live on a main shelf gets pushed to the back and forgotten. Also check the lid. Owners commonly report that lids which pop off while pouring are the most annoying daily flaw, more than flow speed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some households do better with a filter-as-you-pour design, such as Brita Stream, which filters as water passes through the spout so you can pour immediately after filling. The trade-off is that this format is typically certified for chlorine taste and odor rather than lead, so it fits taste-only problems best. The same compromise shows up on faucet-mount filters, which are faster but bulky on the tap.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filter-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When to Change Cartridges
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rated life is the volume over which the cartridge was certified to keep performing. Past that point you have no evidence it still reduces lead or PFAS, even if the water still tastes fine. Taste is a poor gauge because chlorine reduction usually outlasts the health-related media. Treat the gallon rating as a hard limit, not a suggestion, and change sooner if flow slows sharply or the water turns cloudy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most pitchers include a lid indicator. Basic ones are timers that count days since you pressed a button, which is accurate only if your usage matches the brand's assumption of roughly 20 gallons a month. Smarter indicators count lid openings, which tracks actual use better. If you drink more than average, set a phone reminder based on gallons, not months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before first use, almost every cartridge needs a soak or a flush. Carbon fines, harmless black specks, are normal in the first pitcher or two. Discard the first one or two fills as the instructions say, and pre-soak any cartridge that floats or runs slowly from new, because trapped air is the usual cause. Seating the cartridge fully until it clicks prevents bypass, where water runs around the filter instead of through it.
          </p>

