        <div id="pitcher-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What a Pitcher Cannot Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pitcher treats only what you drink and cook with. It does nothing for shower water, laundry, dishwasher spotting or the scale building up in a <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">water heater</a>, and it will not fix the hair and soap buildup behind a slow bathroom sink, which is a job for a <a href="/best-drain-snake/">drain snake</a>. If your complaint is hard water, rust stains or sulfur smell throughout the house, you need a point-of-entry solution: a whole-house filter, a softener or an iron filter installed where the main line enters. That is a different budget and a different purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pitchers do not disinfect. Unless a specific cartridge carries a certified microbiological claim, assume it does not remove bacteria or viruses, and never use one in place of a boil-water notice. Filtered water also loses the residual chlorine that kept it sanitary in the pipes, so keep the pitcher in the fridge and drink the contents within a day or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most pitchers do little for fluoride, nitrate or total dissolved solids. ZeroWater is the main exception for dissolved solids, and a few specialty cartridges claim fluoride reduction, but none matches an RO system. If your water report shows nitrate above the federal limit of 10 milligrams per liter, especially with an infant in the house, use bottled water or a certified RO system until it is fixed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity is the other ceiling. Once a household drinks more than about two gallons a day, refilling a pitcher becomes a chore and cartridge costs climb. At that point an under-sink filter with a dedicated faucet is usually cheaper per gallon and far more convenient. Compare options in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration system for home</a> rankings before buying another multipack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cleaning and Hygiene
          </h2>

          <p>
            A filter pitcher is a damp container that holds water without chlorine, so it can grow a slimy film or green tint if ignored. Wash the reservoir, lid and pitcher body by hand with mild dish soap every time you change the cartridge, and ideally every couple of weeks between changes. Rinse thoroughly so soap residue does not reach the new filter. Most plastic bodies are top-rack dishwasher safe, but check the manual, because high heat can warp lids.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the pitcher out of direct sunlight, which encourages algae, and away from the stove. Store it in the fridge whenever possible. Never filter hot water: carbon cartridges are rated for cold water, and heat can release captured contaminants and damage the media. If you want filtered water for tea or coffee, filter it cold and then boil it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you go on vacation for more than a week, discard the water in the pitcher and leave the cartridge in place. Some brands recommend running a fresh fill through before drinking again. If the cartridge has dried out completely, soak it again as you did when it was new. Owners commonly report that a dried cartridge pours slowly until it is fully re-wetted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recycle old cartridges where you can. Brita runs a free mail-back program through TerraCycle, and ZeroWater has offered a cartridge return credit. Recycling details change, so check the brand website. At several cartridges a year, the waste is small compared with bottled water, but it is still worth avoiding the landfill when a free option exists.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the front of the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The front of the box says reduces up to 99 percent or NSF certified without telling you which contaminants. Certification is listed per contaminant, so a pitcher can be certified for chlorine taste and nothing that affects health. Open the performance data sheet online and look for lead or PFOA/PFOS by name before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running cartridges past their rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water can still taste fine long after a cartridge has stopped reducing lead, because chlorine removal lasts longest. The gallon rating is the only evidence you have that the health claims still hold. Track gallons, not months, if your household drinks more than about 20 gallons a month.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filtering hot water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pitcher cartridges are designed and certified for cold water only. Hot water can damage carbon media and release captured contaminants into the pitcher. Filter cold water, refrigerate it, and heat it afterward for tea, coffee or cooking. The same rule applies to faucet-mount filters. Never run a pitcher under the hot tap to speed it up, either.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the first flush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New cartridges shed harmless carbon dust and can trap air, which causes black specks, floating filters and painfully slow flow. Soak or flush the cartridge as the instructions say and discard the first one or two fills. Seat it until it clicks so water cannot bypass the media.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a pitcher for well water without testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most pitchers are not certified for bacteria, nitrate or arsenic, which are the main private-well risks. Get the well tested through a state-certified lab first. If it shows a problem, a pitcher is the wrong tool, and a certified point-of-use or point-of-entry system is the right one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

